This is a working _non released_ Sysop Activity executable and door that
allows users to automatically know what kind of state the Sysop is in.

SysopActivity = Amiga executable
Sysop_Door    = MAXs door

* NOTE *  This is definately my _cutest_ door.

The executable can be clicked on by the Sysop and one of a number of options
chosen, depending on how the Sysop is feeling.

ie:  The Sysop can be "AVAILABLE"
                      "BUSY"
                      "PISSED" as in angry.
                      "WATCHING"
                      "ASLEEP"
                      "NOT AVAILABLE"

Once the Sysop has chosen an option, a simple data file is updated with that
choice, from then on, a simple door, that the users have access to, will then
display an ANSI that corresponds to the option chosen by the Sysop.

Allows the users to quickly and instantly see if the Sysop wants/can be paged.


You can add/delete as many options as you like, (will require slight code
mods and a re-build), or leave it as it is.  You have complete control
over the ANSIs, the default ones are some quickies that I whipped up. 

To compile the source, you'll need SAS C 6.X.

To install it, just use sumthin' akin to this:

  KEY  FUNCTION  EXTRAS  LOW  HIGH   PATH/FILENAME
   W      34       0     10   10000  DOORS:SysopActivity/Sysop_Door

Copy the #?.text ANSIs into your BBS:Text directory.

Once the above door is installed, the Sysop, (you), are able to run
SysopActivity at any time, (except when a user is running the door),
to change the current Sysop's status.


